  "id": "PSI-102",
  "summary_plain": "The Smoke and Aerosol Measurement Experiment studied how smoke particles and liquid droplets form when common spacecraft materials overheat in microgravity. Conducted inside the Microgravity Science Glovebox on the International Space Station, the investigation burned solid fuels and sample materials to understand why low-gravity smoke behaves differently than on Earth. The findings help engineers design advanced smoke detectors that can reliably protect crewed spacecraft from fire hazards.",
  "summary_expert": "The Smoke and Aerosol Measurement Experiment (SAME) performed flight investigations on the ISS using solid fuels and sample materials including Kapton, Teflon, dibutyl phthalate, lampwick, and silicone. The forced air flow speed (ventilation velocity) in the tests ranged from 5 to 10 cm/s, while variables that changed between tests included flow and fuel type. The documents provided describe the objectives, design, and experimental setup, aiming to quantify smoke particulate sizes and relate droplet size distributions to smoke generation and transport conditions in microgravity.",
  "key_findings": [
    {
      "text": "Liquid smoke particles produced in microgravity can achieve sizes larger than 1 micron, whereas solid particulate remains in the sub-micrometer range.",
